texts": ["Yes, \"Traction\" by Gino Wickman is a well-known book that focuses on the principles of traction investing. Gino Wickman is a renowned investor and entrepreneur who has built a successful career by applying his unique investment strategies. In \"Traction,\" Wickman shares his insights and experiences on how to identify and invest in companies that have strong traction, meaning they have a proven track record of growth and customer satisfaction.\n\nThe book emphasizes the importance of looking beyond traditional financial metrics and instead focusing on the fundamentals of traction, such as customer acquisition, retention, and revenue growth. Wickman", "Creating a Rust application that interacts with the ElevenLabs.io API and reads clipboard contents aloud using text-to-speech (TTS) involves several steps. You'll need to set up the Rust project, integrate with the ElevenLabs API, and use a TTS library to read the clipboard contents aloud. Below is a step-by-step guide to help you get started.\n\n1. **Set Up the Rust Project:**\n   First, create a new Rust project using Cargo, Rust's package manager and build system.\n\n   ```sh\n   cargo new elevenlabs_clipboard_reader\n   cd elevenlabs_clipboard_reader\n   ```\n\n2. **Add Dependencies:**\n   Add the necessary dependencies to your `Cargo.toml` file. You'll need `reqwest` for making HTTP requests, `serde` for JSON serialization/deserialization, `tokio` for async runtime, and `clipboard` for clipboard operations. You may also need a TTS library like `espeak-rs` or `say`.\n\n   ```toml\n   [dependencies]\n   reqwest = { version = \"0.11\", features = [\"json\"] }\n   serde = { version = \"1.0\", features = [\"derive\"] }\n   tokio = { version = \"1\", features = [\"full\"] }\n   clipboard = \"1.0\"\n   espeak-rs = \"0.5\"\n   ```\n\n3. **Set Up the Project Structure:**\n   Create a new module for interacting with the ElevenLabs API and another for handling clipboard operations.\n\n   ```sh\n   mkdir src/api src/clipboard\n   ```\n\n4. **Implement the ElevenLabs API Interaction:**\n   Create a new file `src/api/mod.rs` and implement the necessary functions to interact with the ElevenLabs API.\n\n   ```rust\n   // src/api/mod.rs\n   use reqwest::Client;\n   use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\n\n   #[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]\n   struct ElevenLabsResponse {\n       // Define the structure based on the ElevenLabs API response\n   }\n\n   pub struct ElevenLabsClient {\n       client: Client,\n       api_key: String,\n   }\n\n   impl ElevenLabsClient {\n       pub fn new(api_key: String) -> Self {\n           Self {\n               client: Client::new(),\n               api_key,\n           }\n       }\n\n       pub async fn get_clipboard_content(&self) -> Result<String, reqwest::Error> {\n           let response = self.client\n               .get(\"https://api.elevenlabs.io/clipboard\")\n               .header(\"Authorization\", format!(\"Bearer {}\", self.api_key))\n               .send()\n               .await?;\n\n           response.json::<ElevenLabsResponse>().await?;\n           Ok(response.text().await?)\n       }\n   }\n   ```\n\n5. **Implement Clipboard Reading:**\n   Create a new file `src/clipboard/mod.rs` and implement the clipboard reading functionality.\n\n   ```rust\n   // src/clipboard/mod.rs\n   use clipboard::{ClipboardProvider, ClipboardExt};\n\n   pub async fn read_clipboard() -> Result<String, clipboard::Error> {\n       let mut provider = ClipboardProvider::new()?;\n       let contents = provider.get_contents()?;\n       Ok(contents)\n   }\n   ```\n\n6. **Implement TTS Functionality:**\n   Create a new file `src/tts/mod.rs` and implement the TTS functionality using the `espeak-rs` library.\n\n   ```rust\n   // src/tts/mod.rs\n   use espeak_rs::Espeak;\n\n   pub async fn speak(text: &str) -> Result<(), espeak_rs::Error> {\n       let espeak = Espeak::new();\n       espeak.speak(text).await?;\n       Ok(())\n   }\n   ```\n\n7. **Integrate Everything", "Sure, here is the reformatted \"Filters\" section for better readability:\n\n---\n\n**Filters**\n\n- **Filter by Date Range:**\n  - Start Date: [Select Start Date]\n  - End Date: [Select End Date]\n\n- **Filter by Category:**\n  - Category 1: [Select Category]\n  - Category 2: [Select Category]\n  - Category 3: [Select Category]\n\n- **Filter by Priority:**\n  - High Priority: [Select High Priority]\n  - Medium Priority: [Select Medium Priority]\n  - Low Priority: [Select Low Priority]\n\n- **Filter by Status:**\n  - Open: [Select Open]\n  - In Progress: [Select In Progress]\n  - Closed: [Select Closed]\n\n- **Filter by Assigned To:**\n  - [Select User]\n\n- **Filter by Tags:**\n  - Tag 1: [Select Tag]\n  - Tag 2: [Select Tag]\n  - Tag 3: [Select Tag]\n\n---\n\nThis should make it easier to read and navigate through the filters. Let me know if you need any further adjustments!", "In the context of JIT (Just-In-Time) compilation, a \"push\" typically refers to the process of adding a new method or function to the JIT compiler's knowledge base. This can involve several steps:\n\n1. **Identification**: The JIT compiler identifies a method or function that it hasn't seen before during the execution of the program.\n2. **Analysis**: The JIT compiler analyzes the method or function to understand its structure, behavior, and any potential optimizations that can be applied.\n3. **Compilation**: The JIT compiler generates machine code for the new method or function, often using a combination of static analysis and dynamic profiling to optimize performance.\n4. **Integration**: The newly compiled code is integrated into the running program, allowing the method or function to be executed more efficiently.\n\nIn essence, a \"push\" in this context is about expanding the JIT compiler's capabilities by incorporating new code into its execution environment, thereby", "| Project Charter |  |\n| --- | --- |\n|  | 2. Users may not be satisfied with the functionality or usabilit